      keyTerms: [
        { term: 'Carol Joy Hatton', definition: 'The founder of Carol Joy London, who spent years travelling in search of high-efficacy ingredients and worked with Swiss and German laboratories for close to a decade before launching the brand in 2009.' },
        { term: 'The Dorchester account', definition: 'The brand’s inaugural spa partnership, won in December 2009, which established its spa-first route to market and the standard of spa it partners with.' },
        { term: 'Spa-first brand', definition: 'A house whose products were developed for professional treatment rooms and only afterwards offered for home use; the reverse of a retail brand that later adds a spa menu.' },
        { term: 'Results-driven', definition: 'The register of this house: promises framed as visible, specific changes the guest can see at the mirror, rather than as sensations or states of mind.' },
      ],
      caseStudy: {
        title: 'The comparison question at a Mayfair hotel spa',
        scenario: `Priya is a senior therapist at a Mayfair hotel spa that moved its facial menu to Carol Joy London three months ago. Her 11:00 guest, Mrs Aldridge, has had the same facial with a well-known French house at another London hotel for nine years and has booked here only because her usual spa was full. Before the consultation form is even open she says, pleasantly but firmly: "I am not going to like this as much, am I? I have never heard of them." Priya has perhaps forty seconds before the moment passes.`,
        insight: `Priya does not defend and does not oversell. She gives the story: a British house founded in 2009 by Carol Joy Hatton, who spent years finding the ingredients and close to a decade in Swiss and German laboratories before she would launch it; the first spa to take it was The Dorchester, and it is now in fifteen spas worldwide. Then she makes a specific promise she intends to keep: "It is a collagen house, so what you should notice at the end is lift and plumpness rather than just glow. I will show you in the mirror and you can tell me whether I am right." Mrs Aldridge relaxes, because she has been given a testable claim rather than a sales pitch. The professional lesson is that scepticism is answered with facts and a falsifiable promise. Adjectives make a doubtful guest more doubtful; a story with dates in it, followed by evidence at the mirror, converts.`,
      },
      summary: `Carol Joy London is a British house founded in 2009 by Carol Joy Hatton, built on years of global ingredient sourcing and close to a decade of research with Swiss and German laboratories. Its first partner was The Dorchester, won on a snowy December morning, and that spa-first route to market still defines it: fifteen hand-picked destinations from Monaco to the Maldives, with Fairmont and The Ritz-Carlton alongside The Dorchester. The positioning compresses to one sentence - results-driven luxury skincare, powered by collagen, delivered in the world's most prestigious spas - and it instructs you to consult for the outcome, narrate the actives and finish with visible evidence.`,
      nextStep: `Before your next shift, deliver your thirty-second version of the brand story to a colleague and ask them what they can repeat back. Whatever they cannot repeat is the part you have not yet made memorable.`,
