      keyTerms: [
        { term: 'Native Collagen Fibres', definition: 'Collagen fibres described by the house as 97% identical to human skin, which strengthen the epidermal barrier, boost hydration, soothe the look of irritation and help optimise the bioavailability of surrounding actives.' },
        { term: 'Soluble Collagen Molecules', definition: 'Collagen with an exceptional ability to bind water at the skin’s surface, maintaining hydration, diminishing the appearance of fine lines and enhancing suppleness. The technology behind the Pure Collagen Spray.' },
        { term: 'Collagen Peptides', definition: 'Low molecular weight, skin-mimicking peptides that permeate the epidermal barrier to support collagen synthesis, improving firmness and elasticity and reducing the appearance of wrinkles.' },
        { term: 'Golden Millet Oil', definition: 'The signature Swiss ingredient of the house, cold-pressed by a non-chemical method perfected over twelve years to preserve molecular integrity; rich in oleic acid, omega oils and essential fatty acids, and used for barrier strength and moisture retention.' },
        { term: 'Transepidermal water loss', definition: 'The evaporation of water through the skin barrier. Reducing it is one of the stated functions of Golden Millet Oil and the reason barrier work underpins every other result.' },
        { term: 'The four precious ingredients', definition: 'Golden Millet Oil, collagen, caviar and algae, and 24 carat gold: the group of ingredients the house is known for, and the group that maps onto its treatment menu.' },
      ],
      caseStudy: {
        title: 'The £195 question at the retail wall',
        scenario: `Tom is a therapist at a resort spa on the south coast. After a Collagen and Hyaluronic Acid Facial his guest, Mr Nair, picks up the Ultimate Serum, sees £195 and says, without hostility, "Talk me out of putting this down." He is a pharmacist. He will notice an invented claim instantly, and he will also notice if Tom retreats into vagueness. Tom has about a minute and one product in his hand.`,
        insight: `Tom answers with structure rather than enthusiasm. He explains that the house uses three collagen technologies for three different jobs, and that this serum carries the peptides, the ones small enough to permeate the barrier and support the skin's own collagen synthesis, alongside vitamin C, hyaluronic acid, alpha arbutin, diamond powder and an AHA-rich fruit acid complex. He then draws the boundary himself, before Mr Nair can: no topical serum replaces collagen the body has stopped making, and what this one does is support synthesis and improve firmness, texture and radiance. He finishes by connecting it to the treatment Mr Nair just had. The sale closes, but the more valuable outcome is that a professionally sceptical guest now trusts the spa. The lesson is that with an informed guest, naming your own limits is the strongest possible move. Precision beats persuasion, and volunteering the boundary is what makes the rest of your claims credible.`,
      },
      summary: `The science of this house has two pillars. Three collagen technologies do three jobs: Native Collagen Fibres for the barrier, at 97% identity with human skin; Soluble Collagen Molecules for water binding at the surface; Collagen Peptides, low molecular weight, for synthesis, firmness and elasticity. Golden Millet Oil, Swiss, cold-pressed by a non-chemical method perfected over twelve years, does the barrier work that lets everything else perform. Around them sit hyaluronic acid, vitamin C, alpha arbutin, diamond powder, hibiscus flower acids, fruit acid complex, titanium dioxide and Swiss Apple Stem Cell Extract, and the four precious ingredients the house is known for: Golden Millet Oil, collagen, caviar and algae, and 24 carat gold. Use the collagen decline figures to explain the need, and never claim a topical product replaces what the body has lost.`,
      nextStep: `On your next shift, narrate one collagen technology by name during a treatment, at the moment the guest can feel the product going on, and note what she asks you afterwards.`,
