      caseStudy: {
        title: 'The wedding in nine days',
        scenario: `Laura is covering an agency shift at a country house hotel spa carrying Carol Joy London. Her 15:00 is a sixty-minute Classic Facial for Mrs Doherty, who mentions during consultation that her daughter marries in nine days, that she is dreading the close-up photographs of her hands holding the bouquet, and that she has barely slept for a fortnight. The spa is quiet on Thursday and the coordinator has said so. Laura has three genuine openings and eight minutes of consultation.`,
        insight: `Laura raises one, not three. She keeps the Classic Facial as booked because a sensitive first-time guest nine days from a wedding is not the moment for a new advanced protocol. During the treatment she mentions, once, that there is a sixty-minute Collagen Sculpting Treatment that works the eyes, lips, décolleté and hands specifically, and that a week before the wedding is good timing. She says nothing about the Deep Sleep Ritual until the goodbye, where she mentions it as an idea for the night before the wedding rather than a sale today. Mrs Doherty books the Sculpting for the following Tuesday and, at the desk, adds the Deep Sleep Ritual for the Friday. Two bookings, no pressure, both tied to something the guest volunteered. The lesson is that menu knowledge converts through restraint: the therapist who names one right treatment at the right moment outsells the one who lists four.`,
      },
      summary: `The menu is five groups: collagen-boosting technology treatments, comprehensive collagen treatments, advanced rejuvenation, body and massage. Almost every group offers a thirty, a sixty and a ninety, so every booking has an honest next rung. Know your add-on inventory (Sculpting Eye Lift, Body Polish, Nourishing Scalp Massage), your sampler (Express Collagen Facial) and your flagship (Caviar and Collagen Cocoon). Build your own map from the spa's own menu on day one, including durations as sold and which technologies you are certified on, and flag any gap to the coordinator before a booking finds you.`,
      nextStep: `Today, write out the five groups from memory, then check yourself against your spa's menu and note every treatment you could not place. Those are the ones a guest will ask about first.`,
